COMMAND NAME: gpdbupgrade

Upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 3.0 system to 3.1.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpdbupgrade -u {3.1 | 3.0} [-d <master_data_directory>] [-N] [-c] 
[-s] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>] 
[-D] [-?]

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpdbupgrade utility upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 
3.0 system to 3.1. It can also be used to downgrade a 3.1 system 
back to 3.0. It updates the system catalog and the software version 
number, but not the actual software binaries.

If your Greenplum Database system has a standby master host configured, 
the gpdbupgrade utility will not upgrade the standby master. It will 
remove the standby master from your system configuration and then you 
must re-add it back to your system after the upgrade is complete.

To upgrade from 3.0 to 3.1 follow these general steps:

1. Log in to your Greenplum master host as the Greenplum 
   superuser (gpadmin).

2. Stop your current Greenplum Database 3.0.0.x system:
    gpstop

3. Run the Greenplum Database 3.1.0.0 installer. When prompted, 
   choose an installation location in the same base directory as 
   your 3.0 installation. For example: 
    /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0

4. Edit the environment of the Greenplum Database superuser 
  (gpadmin) and make sure you are sourcing the greenplum_path.sh 
  file for the 3.1 installation. For example change the following 
  line in .bashrc or your chosen profile file:
   source /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.0.0.x
     to
   source /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0

5. Source the environment file you just edited. For example:
    source ~/.bashrc

6. Install the 3.1.0.0 binaries on all of the segment hosts. 
   You can tar the Greenplum installation directory, use gpscp 
   to copy it to the segment hosts, then use gpssh to untar it at 
   the segment hosts. For example:
     tar -cvf /home/gpadmin/gp.tar /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0
     gpscp -f seghostname_file -u root /home/gpadmin/gp.tar =:/ 
     gpssh -f seghostname_file -u root
            => gtar --directory / -xvf /gp.tar
            => chown -R gpadmin /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0
            => rm /gp.tar 

7. Run the upgrade utility:
    gpdbupgrade -u 3.1

Additional steps for upgrading your standby master host for 
systems that have master mirroring configured:

8. On your standby master host, run the installer to install 
   the 3.1.0.0 binaries. When prompted, choose an installation 
   location in the same base directory as your 3.0 installation. 
   For example: 
     /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0

9. Edit the environment of the Greenplum Database superuser 
   (gpadmin) and make sure you are sourcing the greenplum_path.sh 
   file for the 3.1 installation. For example change the following 
   line in .bashrc or your chosen profile file:
     source /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.0.0.x
       to
     source /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.1.0.0

10. Source the environment file you just edited. For example:
      source ~/.bashrc

11. Return to your primary master host machine and run the 
    gpinitstandby utility. This will re-add the standby master 
    host to your newly upgraded Greenplum Database system.
      gpinitstandby -s standby_masterhost

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

-u 3.1 | 3.0

Required. Specifies the version to upgrade (or downgrade) to. 
For users currently on 3.0, you can upgrade to 3.1. For users 
who have already upgraded to 3.1, there is also the option to 
downgrade back to 3.0.


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, 
the value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-N (upgrade pg_control)

Do not upgrade the pg_control files used by the write-ahead 
log (WAL). The aim of WAL is to ensure that a log is written 
before database records are altered. After a checkpoint has 
been made and the log is flushed, the checkpoint’s position is 
saved in the file pg_control. The default is to allow the 
upgrade utility to modify pg_control.


-c (backup catalog first)

Creates a backup of the system catalog schema before performing 
the upgrade. The default is to not back up the system catalogs.


-s (stop system after upgrade)

Shuts down Greenplum Database after the upgrade is 
complete. The default is to leave the newly upgraded system 
up and running.


-B parallel_processes

The number of segments to upgrade in parallel. If not specified, 
the utility will start up to 60 parallel processes depending 
on how many segment instances it needs to upgrade.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the 
screen, but is still written to the log file.


-l logfile_directory

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************


Upgrade from Greenplum Database version 3.0 to version 3.1:

gpdbupgrade -u 3.1 -d /dbfast1/gpdb-1


